expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2830:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1327:./theory/aoyagi-2023-reproduction/aoyagi-2023-worked.tex:854:$\tfrac12(\sum_i q_i^2-\sum_k m_k^2)$, and \emph{matches the paper's total $L=2$ RLCT on all
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2831:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1328:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.bbl:49:M.~Aoyagi.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2832:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1332:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:109:tools from the theory of quiver representations. In particular, we determine its codimension $\codimnot$ and the number $\theta$ of its top-dimensional irreducible components. Our solution is presented in three forms: a Poincar\'e series in equivariant cohomology, a quadratic integer program, and an explicit formula. In the course of the proof, we establish a surprising property: $\codimnot$ and $\theta$ are invariant under arbitrary permutations of the dimension vector. We also show that the real log-canonical threshold of the function taking a tuple to the square Frobenius norm of its product is $\codimnot/2$. These results are motivated by the study of deep linear neural networks in machine learning and Bayesian statistics (singular learning theory) and show that deep linear networks are in a certain sense ``mildly singular".
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2833:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1333:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:132:Describing $\Sigma$'s components or even the multiset of their dimensions seems to be a challenging combinatorial problem. In this paper, we determine the codimension $\codimnot=\codim_{\Rep}\Sigma$ of $\Sigma$ in $\Rep$---or equivalently the codimension of its largest dimensional irreducible components---and the number $\theta$ of such top-dimensional components. 
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2834:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1334:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:133:%The number $\codimnot$ answers the following question from elementary linear algebra: {\em what is the largest dimension of a parametric family of tuples of matrices which multiply to 0?} Similarly, $\theta$ answers the question {\em how many `main' ways are there for a tuple of matrices to multiply to 0?} we don;t agree about this, so let us just leave it out.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2835:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1335:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:146:The main results of the paper are formulas for $\codimnot$ and $\theta$. We have three in the main body of the paper, (Theorems~\ref{thm:Pseries}, \ref{thm:QIP}, and \ref{thm:main-codim}) and one in the appendix (Theorem~\ref{thm:UsingAvoidingIdeal}).
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2836:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1336:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:148:Theorem~\ref{thm:Pseries} uses Poincar\'e series calculations in equivariant cohomology, and produces an explicit formal power series in $q$ whose lowest-degree term is $\theta q^\codimnot$. The resulting formula implies, in particular, that neither $\codimnot$ nor $\theta$ depend on the ordering of the integers $d_0,d_1,\ldots,d_N$. This is a priori surprising, since reordering the $d_i$'s can change the dimension of $\Rep$ and drastically alter both the number of components of $\Sigma$ and the multiset of their codimensions. We expect there should be an elementary algebraic proof of this invariance, without topology. However, we do not provide such a proof here, relying instead on Theorem~\ref{thm:Pseries} (an alternative argument, still relying on equivariant cohomology, is given in Appendix~\ref{sec:app_theta}). A key component of our Poincar\'e series calculation is a remarkable result from Gabriel's theory of quivers: the indecomposable module corresponding to the longest root in equioriented type A quivers is both projective and injective.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2837:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1337:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:150:In Theorem~\ref{thm:QIP}, we present $\codimnot$ as the optimal solution of a quadratic integer program and $\theta$ as the number of such optimal solutions. The proof relies on the combinatorics and algebra of quivers, as well as the crucial invariance of $\codimnot$ and $\theta$ with respect to the ordering of the integers $d_0,d_1,\ldots,d_N$, as discussed earlier.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2838:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1338:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:152:In Theorem~\ref{thm:main-codim}, we provide our most explicit formulas for $\codimnot$ and $\theta$, which are well-suited for asymptotic analysis. The proof hinges on reinterpreting the quadratic integer program as a minimal distance problem: finding the closest lattice point in a simplex to a given point in Euclidean space. Central to this problem is the solution to the closest vector problem for type A root lattices, where the Voronoi cells of these lattices have the expected structure.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2839:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1339:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:156:One motivation for studying the geometry of composable tuples of matrices is \emph{deep linear (neural) networks} and specifically their statistical properties in Bayesian statistics. We give a quick overview here and refer to the introduction of Section \ref{sec:RLCT} for more context.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2840:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1340:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:168:The \emph{exact, theoretical} computation of $\rlct(K)$ in realistic machine learning scenarios is generally impossible. However, for deep linear networks, $\rlct(K)$ has been calculated by M.~Aoyagi \cite{aoyagi} and this was used in \cite{lau2024locallearningcoefficientsingularityaware} to calibrate the accuracy of the local learning coefficient. The original formula of Aoyagi is complicated, and we started this project in order to understand it better. Combined with our results, it takes a rather simple form (Theorem \ref{thm:aoyagi-rlct}):
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2841:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1343:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:861:    When $k=\R$, one can also ask about the irreducible components of $\cSigma^r_{\ud}$ as a \emph{real analytic set}. Those are not in general the same as the algebraically irreducible components, because the real points of the orbits $\CO_{\um}$ can be disconnected. So in particular one should be careful about how to interpret the number $\theta$ over $\R$. However by Theorem~\ref{thm:base_field}, we know that all algebraically irreducible components have real points, and that those real points have the same codimension as the algebraic codimension, so the number $C$ is unambiguous.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2842:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1344:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:915:     Let $0\leq r\leq\min\ud$, let $C$ be the codimension of $\cSigma^r_{\ud} \subset \Rep_{\ud}$ and $\theta$ the number of its top-dimensional irreducible components. Then  
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2843:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1345:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:916:    \[Q^r_{\ud}=\theta q^C+\text{higher order terms}.\]
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2844:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1346:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:924:In the theory of characteristic classes of singularities---not discussed in this paper except in Appendix~\ref{sec:app_theta}---the sum $\sum_{s=0}^r Q^s_{\ud}$ is the Poincar\'e series of the ideal of cohomology classes ``universally supported'' on $\cSigma_{\ud}^r$.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2845:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1347:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:938:Combined with Lemma~\ref{lem:Qs_codim}, this Theorem provides formulas for $C$ and $\theta$ which are not easy to write in closed form but can be implemented efficiently in a computer algebra system. 
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2846:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1348:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1115:    The numbers $C$, $\theta$ are invariant under permutations of~$\ud$.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2847:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1349:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1122:In Appendix~\ref{sec:app_theta} we will give another proof of this invariance. We do not know of a proof relying solely on equation \eqref{eqn:dim_formula} and the combinatorics of Kostant partitions. Note that given a permutation $\sigma(\ud)$ of $\ud$, the sets of Kostant partitions $\CM^+_{\ud}$ and $\CM^+_{\sigma(\ud)}$ do not necessarily have the same cardinality, and in particular $\sigma$ does not induce a bijection between them.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2848:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1351:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1136:While Theorem \ref{thm:Pseries} is a very effective way of calculating the number of top-dimensional components of $\Sigma^0_{\ud}$, it is useless for asymptotic analysis. Hence, it is desirable to have more explicit formulas. Towards such, we first reduce the problem of calculating $\codimnot$ and $\theta$ to a quadratic integer program. The argument is {\em almost} independent of Section~\ref{sec:PoincareSection}; almost, because, in the case when the dimension vector $\ud$ is not weakly increasing, we use Corollary~\ref{cor:PermutationInvariance}. As we saw above, it is enough to treat the case $r=0$ and we do so in this section.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2849:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1360:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1160:  For $\ud=(2,2,3)$ the \eqref{eq:QIP} is minimizing the function $G_{\ud}(\ue)=e_1^2+e_1e_2+e_2^2+e_2$ on the three-element set $\{(0,2),(1,1),(2,0)\}$. The minimum is 4, attained at the second and third element of the set. Hence $\theta=2$, $C=4$, cf. Examples \ref{ex:222324}, \ref{ex:q-series}.  
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2850:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1366:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1270:  The combinatorial description of the $\theta$ top dimensional components of $\Sigma^0_{\ud}$ for arbitrary $\ud$ (not necessarily weakly increasing) will be given in \cite{KR}.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2851:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1382:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1778:    Informally we see in both cases that the magnitude of $\codim \Sigma_{\ud}^0$ is not too sensitive to $N$ (``the depth''), only to $d$ (``the width'') of the quiver (``network''), cf. Section \ref{sec:RLCT}.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2852:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1383:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1782:\section{Real-log canonical threshold of deep linear networks} %\\and Aoyagi's formula}
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2853:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1384:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1783:\label{sec:RLCT}
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2854:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1385:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1785:The initial motivation of this work was to better understand the work of Aoyagi \cite{aoyagi} and to relate it to the theory of quiver representations. Aoyagi's work takes place in the context of \emph{singular learning theory}, the Bayesian statistics of singular statistical models \cite{watanabe2009,watanabe2018,watanabe2024recent}. In singular learning theory, the \emph{real log-canonical threshold (rlct)}, a geometric invariant of singularities of real analytic functions, plays a central role because it controls the asymptotic performance of Bayesian inference.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2855:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1386:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1790:        \item The \emph{real log canonical threshold} (RLCT) of $F$ is
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2856:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1387:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1874:In \cite{aoyagi}, Aoyagi computes the real log-canonical threshold of the relative entropy function of \emph{deep linear neural networks}. Deep linear networks are obtained from standard (feedforward, fully-connected) deep linear networks by replacing their non-linear activation functions by linear maps. Despite their simplicity, they are a useful ``toy model" in modern deep learning theory, as we discussed in Section \ref{sec:related}. By definition, the weights of a deep linear networks are a tuple of composable matrices, and the function computed by the network is their product, so the parameter space of the model is $\Rep_{\ud}$ where $\ud\in \N^{N+1}$ records the widths of the layers and $N$ is the depth of the network. We also need a ``true distribution" generating the data, or rather in this context a ``true function". Aoyagi makes the assumption that this true function is linear and given by some $B\in \Mat_{d_N,d_0}^{\rk=r}$ with $0\leq r\leq \min{\ud}$. This condition on the rank emsures that $B\in \mathrm{Im}(\mult)$; in statistical terminology, we are in the \emph{realisable} or \emph{well-specified} case.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2857:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1389:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1941:\section{Another algebraic characterization of $C$ and $\theta$.} \label{sec:app_theta}
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2858:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1390:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1944:We showed three efficient ways to calculate the dimension of top-dimensional components of $\Sigma^r_{\ud}$: Theorems \ref{thm:Pseries}, \ref{thm:QIP}, and \ref{thm:main-codim}. In this section---as an added bonus---we give another characterization (of both $C$ and $\theta$) that is less calculationally efficient than the other three. However, it suggests some relations with the so-called interpolation method for characteristic classes of singularities.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2859:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1391:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1971:As before, the number and codimension of the top-dimensional components of $\Sigma_{\ud}^r$ are denoted by $\theta$ and $C$.
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2860:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1392:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1974:    The degree and rank of the lowest degree part of $\CA^r_{\ud}$ are $C$ and $\theta$.  
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2861:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1394:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1990:Calculation shows that the lowest degree part of this ideal is of degree 4, and of rank~2. In fact, it is spanned by the {\em two} ($\Rightarrow \theta=2$) degree {\em four} ($\Rightarrow C=4$) polynomials
expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/03-theta-h1-h2/codex/order-vs-components-stdout.log:2862:./expeditions/2026-06-25-theta-components/threads/01-theta-adjudication/codex/consult-stdout.log:1395:./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:2026:Clearly, Theorem \ref{thm:UsingAvoidingIdeal} is less computationally efficient than our earlier theorems calculating $C$ and $\theta$. Nevertheless, it (just like Theorem \ref{thm:Pseries}) also displays the a priori non-obvious fact that $C$ and $\theta$ are invariant under the permutation of the components of $\ud$ (cf.~Corollary~\ref{cor:PermutationInvariance}). 
